---

## Solution

We assume $n \geq 2$ (the case $n=1$ is degenerate and treated in a remark at the end).

**Claim.** The polynomials $P \in \mathbb{R}[X]$ satisfying $P(A) \in O_n(\mathbb{R})$ for every $A \in O_n(\mathbb{R})$ are exactly
$$P(X) = \varepsilon\, X^{k}, \qquad \varepsilon \in \{+1,-1\},\; k \in \mathbb{N}_0.$$

---

### 1. Necessity

Write $P(X)=\sum_{i=0}^{d} a_i X^i$ with $a_d\neq 0$. For $A\in O_n$ we have $A^{T}=A^{-1}$, and since the coefficients are real,
$$P(A)^{T}=P(A^{T})=P(A^{-1}).$$
The condition $P(A)\in O_n$ reads $P(A)^{T}P(A)=I$, i.e.
$$P(A^{-1})\,P(A)=I \qquad\text{for all } A\in O_n. \tag{1}$$

Expanding,
$$P(A^{-1})P(A)=\Big(\sum_{i} a_i A^{-i}\Big)\Big(\sum_{j} a_j A^{j}\Big)=\sum_{i,j} a_i a_j\, A^{\,j-i}=\sum_{m\in\mathbb{Z}} c_m\, A^{m},$$
where $c_m:=\sum_{i} a_i\, a_{i+m}$ (with $a_i=0$ outside $[0,d]$). Note $c_{-m}=c_m$. Thus (1) becomes
$$\sum_{m\in\mathbb{Z}} c_m\, A^{m}=I \qquad\text{for all } A\in O_n. \tag{2}$$

**Step 1 – isolating the constant term.** Taking $A=I$ in (2) gives $\sum_m c_m=1$. We will show below that in fact $c_m=0$ for every $m\neq 0$; then $c_0=1$ follows.

**Step 2 – using rotations to force $c_m=0$ for $m\neq 0$.** Since $n\geq 2$, for every $\theta\in\mathbb{R}$ the block-diagonal matrix
$$A_\theta=\operatorname{diag}\!\big(R_\theta,\, I_{n-2}\big),\qquad R_\theta=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\ \sin\theta&\cos\theta\end{pmatrix},$$
belongs to $O_n$. Substituting $A_\theta$ into (2) and looking at the upper-left $2\times 2$ block yields
$$\sum_{m\neq 0} c_m\, R_{m\theta}=0 \qquad\text{for all }\theta.$$
Reading the $(1,1)$ and $(2,1)$ entries,
$$\sum_{m\neq 0} c_m\cos(m\theta)=0,\qquad \sum_{m\neq 0} c_m\sin(m\theta)=0\qquad\text{for all }\theta.$$
The family $\{\cos(m\theta),\sin(m\theta):m\geq 1\}$ is linearly independent on $\mathbb{R}$, so $c_m=0$ for every $m\neq 0$. Consequently
$$c_0=\sum_{i=0}^{d} a_i^{2}=1,\qquad c_m=\sum_{i} a_i a_{i+m}=0\;\;(m\neq 0). \tag{3}$$

**Step 3 – from the autocorrelation condition to $P=\pm X^{k}$.** Consider the polynomial $Q(z)=\sum_{i=0}^{d} a_i z^{i}\in\mathbb{R}[z]$. For $z=e^{i\theta}$ on the unit circle,
$$|Q(e^{i\theta})|^{2}=Q(e^{i\theta})\,\overline{Q(e^{i\theta})}=\sum_{i,j}a_i a_j\, e^{i(i-j)\theta}=\sum_{m} c_m\, e^{im\theta}=c_0=1,$$
using (3). Hence $|Q(z)|=1$ for all $|z|=1$.

Let $R(z)=z^{d}Q(1/z)=\sum_{i=0}^{d} a_i z^{d-i}$ be the reversed polynomial (real coefficients). On $|z|=1$ we have $\overline{Q(z)}=z^{-d}R(z)$, so
$$1=|Q(z)|^{2}=Q(z)\,z^{-d}R(z)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad Q(z)\,R(z)=z^{d}\quad\text{on }|z|=1.$$
Both sides are polynomials, so the identity holds in $\mathbb{C}[z]$:
$$Q(z)\,R(z)=z^{d}. \tag{4}$$
The right-hand side is a monomial, so each factor on the left must itself be a monomial: $Q(z)=\alpha z^{k}$ and $R(z)=\beta z^{d-k}$ with $\alpha\beta=1$. But $R$ is the reverse of $Q$, so $\beta=\alpha$, giving $\alpha^{2}=1$, i.e. $\alpha=\pm 1$. Therefore
$$P(X)=Q(X)=\pm X^{k}$$
for some $k\in\{0,1,\dots,d\}$.

---

### 2. Sufficiency

Conversely, let $P(X)=\varepsilon X^{k}$ with $\varepsilon\in\{+1,-1\}$ and $k\geq 0$. For any $A\in O_n$,
$$P(A)^{T}P(A)=(\varepsilon A^{k})^{T}(\varepsilon A^{k})=A^{kT}A^{k}=(A^{T})^{k}A^{k}=(A^{-1})^{k}A^{k}=I,$$
so $P(A)\in O_n$.

---

### 3. Remark on $n=1$

When $n=1$, $O_1=\{+1,-1\}$ and the condition reduces to $P(1),P(-1)\in\{+1,-1\}$, which admits infinitely many polynomials (e.g. $P(X)=X$, $P(X)=X^{2}$, $P(X)=1+X-X^{2}$, …). The clean classification above is specific to $n\geq 2$.

---

**Conclusion (for $n\geq 2$).** The required polynomials are precisely $P(X)=X^{k}$ and $P(X)=-X^{k}$ for $k\in\mathbb{N}_{0}$.

